  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25=0,$ find the remaining solutions.\n\n$x=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $1$ | $100$ | $25$ |\n|  | $\u2193$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $4$ | $0$ | $100$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}+100).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}+100=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =-100 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(5\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm 5\\text{i}.$",
